# Authentication setup

This scaffold provides an application-owned `Principal` and an `AuthModule`
composition point connected to the bootstrap. The base API builds and runs
before you implement authentication. Existing routes keep their access rules.

To add authentication:

1. Define the credentials and verified identity types required by your application.
2. Implement `securityhttp.CredentialExtractor[Credentials]`,
   `security.Authenticator[Credentials, Identity]` and
   `security.PrincipalResolver[Identity, auth.Principal]`.
3. Annotate their constructors with `//pfw:coconut`. In `AuthModule`, register
   `pfw.Constructors(securityhttp.NewMiddleware[Credentials, Identity, auth.Principal])`
   using the appropriate imports and your actual application types.
4. Inject `securityhttp.HandlerMiddleware` (stdlib) or
   `securityhttp.RequestAuthenticator` (Echo) and explicitly protect chosen routes.
   Add `securityhttp.ErrorRules()` to your HTTP error mapper for security responses.
5. Run `go tool pfw generate -env local` and test your application's security flow.

The framework supplies contracts and the middleware. JWT, API keys, external
providers, configuration, token issuance and persistence belong to the application.
No authentication implementation, auth endpoints, secrets or dependencies are
installed. Extend `Principal` or replace it to match your application's model.

Use `pfw.Bind` to select a custom middleware implementation or
`pfw.Override(pfw.Constructors(...))` to prefer an alternative constructor.
When several authentication flows satisfy the same middleware interface, choose
explicit bindings or distinct typed dependencies to avoid ambiguous injection.

Repeating `pfw add auth` is a no-op; it preserves an already installed module
and does not migrate previous auth scaffolds.
